{{~##
 Every record type as an incomplete type, in one header.

 C++'s answer to the cross-reference problem, and the same one C uses. A record holding a
 whole-row reference has a member of type `const OtherRecord*`, and a pointer needs only an
 incomplete type - so no table header includes another and two tables pointing at each other
 is not an include cycle.

 In one header rather than each table header declaring what it needs, because that keeps the
 declaration in one place and one order. C++ tolerates a repeated declaration where C99 does
 not, so this is a readability choice here rather than a correctness one - but it is the same
 file for the same reason, and the two targets are better off answering it identically.

 The encryption key is here for a reason that follows from the same layout. It belongs to the
 model rather than to any one table, and the natural place to say so would be the accessor -
 but a table's `read` is defined inline in a header the accessor includes, so at the point
 that call is compiled the accessor does not exist yet. This is the one header every generated
 file in the model already shares, so it is the only place a declaration can be that both the
 tables and the accessor can see.
##~}}
{{ include 'cpp-file-head.sbn' ~}}
{{~ for line in records ~}}
struct {{ line }};
{{~ end ~}}

/// The key the table files were sealed with, or empty when they were not sealed.
///
/// One decision the consuming project makes once, at start-up, before the first table is
/// read - so it is set here rather than passed to every `read`, which would be the same
/// value written out at every call site, and every call site is somewhere it could be wrong.
///
/// Thirty-two bytes, the key the converter was given. Empty - the default - is a project
/// that does not encrypt, and it costs that project nothing: the load path opens the
/// envelope either way, and a file that is not encrypted comes back from that unchanged. A
/// project that does encrypt and leaves this empty gets the reader's own message saying the
/// file is encrypted and no key was given, rather than a failure somewhere inside the format.
///
/// Where the key comes from is the one thing about this layer worth being careful with. It
/// is a secret only until it reaches a repository, a build log or an issue, and it cannot be
/// taken back afterwards - so it is read at start-up from wherever this project keeps
/// secrets, not written into a source file. What the layer is for, and therefore what is and
/// is not worth defending, is spec/wire/tcb-v104-composed-encodings.md section 4.
inline std::vector<std::uint8_t> encryption_key;

/// The key the table files were signed with, or empty when they were not signed.
///
/// A different key from `encryption_key` and independent of it: a file can be signed
/// without being encrypted, and encrypted without being signed.
///
/// What it buys is the thing encryption does not. The cipher is a keystream XOR, so a bit
/// flipped in the file is a bit flipped in the value it decrypts to - no key needed - and
/// every structural check the format makes still passes, because a four-byte float accepts
/// any four bytes. With this set, a file altered after export stops loading instead.
///
/// Setting it makes the reader stricter in a second way: a file that carries no MAC is
/// refused, because otherwise the check could be removed by zeroing sixteen bytes. So the
/// order to turn this on in is data first, key second. spec/wire/tcb-mac-and-signature.md.
inline std::vector<std::uint8_t> mac_key;

/// Whether to check the MAC at all. True by default.
///
/// For tools that read exported files without the key, and for measuring what the check
/// costs at load. It is not a security boundary - anyone who can flip a flag in a shipped
/// binary can read `mac_key` out of the same binary.
inline bool verify_mac = true;
